.TH EDS 1 "2026" "eds" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
eds \- erl_data_shift: a standalone Postgres migration CLI
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B eds
.I command
.RI [ args ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B eds
is a single-binary command-line tool for running and managing Postgres
schema migrations. It reads connection details from a
.B .env
file in the current directory and requires no separately installed runtime.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B con_check
Tests Postgres connectivity using your .env credentials.
.TP
.B stat
Shows table names, row counts, and storage size, largest first.
.TP
.B history
Shows applied migrations: time-since-applied, who applied or reverted
each one, and drift between local migration files and the database.
.TP
.B migrate
Runs all pending .sql files from ./migrations transactionally, one
migration per transaction.
.TP
.B migrate dry-run
Lists pending migrations without applying them.
.TP
.B migrate down
Rolls back the most recently applied migration, using its matching
.I *.down.sql
file.
.TP
.BI "migrate -f " path
Same as
.BR migrate ,
but points to a custom migrations directory instead of ./migrations.
Also accepts
.BI "--path " path
as a longer alias.
.TP
.BI "new " name
Scaffolds a new numbered up+down migration file pair in the migrations
directory, e.g.
.IR 0004_add_users.sql " and " 0004_add_users.down.sql .
.TP
.B init
Scaffolds ./migrations and .env.example in the current directory.
Never overwrites existing files.
.TP
.B --version
Prints the eds version.
.TP
.BR --help ", " -h
Shows a short command summary.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.B eds
reads the following keys from a
.B .env
file in the current directory (see
.B .env.example
after running
.BR "eds init" ):
.TP
.B PG_HOST, PG_PORT, PG_USER, PG_PASSWORD, PG_DATABASE
Required. Standard Postgres connection parameters.
.TP
.B PG_SSLMODE
Optional. One of
.BR disable ", " require ", " verify-ca ", " verify-full .
Defaults to
.B disable
(plaintext) if omitted.
.SH CONCURRENCY
Only one
.B migrate
or
.B "migrate down"
run can execute against a given database at a time. This is enforced
with a Postgres advisory lock; a second concurrent run fails cleanly
with a "migration locked" message rather than racing the first.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B eds
exits non-zero if the requested command fails (e.g. a migration errors,
the database is unreachable, or .env is missing/invalid). It exits 0
on success, including "no pending migrations" style no-ops.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I .env
Connection credentials for the current project. Never committed to
version control.
.TP
.I .env.example
Template with placeholder values, safe to commit.
.TP
.I migrations/
Default directory for numbered .sql migration files and their
.I *.down.sql
counterparts.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Scaffold a new project:
.B eds init
.TP
Create a new migration:
.B eds new add_users_table
.TP
Preview what would run:
.B eds migrate dry-run
.TP
Apply pending migrations:
.B eds migrate
.TP
Undo the last one:
.B eds migrate down
.SH DISCLAIMER
This tool modifies your database schema and data. You are solely
responsible for backing up your data before running any migration.
The authors accept no liability for data loss or downtime resulting
from its use.
.SH SEE ALSO
Full documentation and source: https://github.com/seyed/erl_data_shift
